/*
An Odin implementation of BML (Bridge Markup Language) - the `.bml` notation used for contract
bridge bidding system notes - and its rendering to html.

	doc := bml.parse(source, {resolve_include = read_include})
	defer bml.destroy(doc)
	html := bml.render_html(doc)

# The reference

The python in `enerqi/bml` (a fork of `Kungsgeten/bml`) is the reference implementation and stays
that way. The goal here is BYTE-IDENTICAL html for the whole bridge corpus - `just parity` renders
all 19 files with both and compares - because the published pages were generated by the reference,
so any divergence is a visible regression. Where the code below looks strange, the reason is usually
that the bytes have to match; those places say so individually.

`~/dev/bml/ARCHITECTURE.md` explains how BML parsing works and why. `ARCHITECTURE.md` beside this
file explains this PORT: what differs, what is bug-compatible on purpose, and how to debug a parity
failure.

# Why it exists

Two things the reference cannot do:

  - It renders a FILE. `content_from_file` takes a path, so a live preview of text someone is still
    typing has nothing to hand it. `parse` here takes source text, and `#INCLUDE` is resolved
    through a callback rather than by opening paths relative to the working directory.
  - It keeps its state in MODULE GLOBALS, so converting several documents means one process each -
    which is exactly what the notes build does. Here a parse owns its state.

Speed followed: 1.9 ms for a chapter against 29 ms plus interpreter startup plus a process spawn.

# Layout

	parse.odin       the entry point: newline normalisation, includes, comments, block splitting
	content.odin     Content_Kind, Block, Diagram, and the ORDERED classifier - order is load-bearing
	blocks.odin      the per-kind splitters: list, enum, pipe table, auction table, deal diagram
	bidtree.odin     bid table rows to a tree, and what counts as a row rather than a wrapped line
	directives.odin  #CUT / #COPY / #PASTE / #HIDE / #BIDTABLE
	node.odin        Node, child creation, sequences
	call.odin        bid_representation: one bid token to the auction it stands for
	bids/            `markup:bids`, the call MODEL - a port of the bml tools' `bmlbids.py` (what a call is, how
	                 calls order). A separate package: the parser does not use it; the notes repo's quiz does.
	inline.odin      inline markup (suits, emphasis, links) to element nodes
	xml.odin         an ElementTree emulation, existing purely to serialise like python does
	html.odin        document assembly, in the reference's order

Nothing imports outside `core:`. There is no regular expression engine, and not for want of one:
the reference relies on lookaround in eight places, and neither `core:text/regex` nor rure supports
it - linear-time automata engines give it up by construction. Those patterns need rewriting either
way, so the scanners are hand-written, which keeps the library vendorable as pure Odin and makes it
a single pass instead of the reference's thirty.

# Memory

Everything a `Document` points at lives in one arena that it owns, so `destroy` is a wholesale free
and re-parsing on every keystroke costs a pointer reset. A `Block`, a `Node` or a `string` taken out
of a document dies with it - copy before destroying.

# Not ported

The LaTeX and BSS back ends. Either would be a new file beside `html.odin` and no parser change.
*/
package bml
